<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 128, 350, 145]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.4 Capacity Management  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 153, 261, 169]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.4.1 Overview  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 177, 883, 223]]<|/det|>
Capacity Management is a capability for organizing physical media into Endurance Groups and NVM Sets. There are two different forms of Capacity Management, Fixed Capacity Management and Variable Capacity Management. A controller that supports Capacity Management shall support at least one form.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 228, 760, 245]]<|/det|>
Capacity Management commands shall not be supported by Exported NVM Subsystems.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 250, 883, 295]]<|/det|>
The host uses Fixed Capacity Management to create Endurance Groups and NVM Sets by selecting a configuration which explicitly allocates Media Units (refer to section 8.1.4.2) to Endurance Groups and NVM Sets.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 302, 475, 318]]<|/det|>
The host uses Variable Capacity Management to:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[173, 324, 700, 387]]<|/det|>
create a single Endurance Group by specifying the desired capacity; create a single NVM Set by specifying the desired capacity; delete a single Endurance Group; and delete a single NVM Set.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 392, 883, 438]]<|/det|>
The Capacity Adjustment Factor is the ratio between the capacity consumed by an Endurance Group from the Unallocated NVM Capacity field in the Identify Controller data structure and the total NVM capacity in that Endurance Group, i.e.:  

<|ref|>equation<|/ref|><|det|>[[213, 444, 782, 478]]<|/det|>
\[Capacity Adjustment Factor = INTEGER \(\left(\frac{\text{Capacity Consumed}}{\text{Endurance Group Capacity}} * 100\right)\)\]  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 483, 883, 513]]<|/det|>
(E.g., the value 200 indicates that creation of an Endurance Group with a total NVM capacity of 5 GiB consumes 10 GiB of the Unallocated NVM Capacity indicated by the controller).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 519, 883, 536]]<|/det|>
If an Endurance Group is created, then the controller performs the following actions as an atomic operation:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 541, 883, 601]]<|/det|>
a) the value indicated by the Unallocated NVM Capacity (UNVMCAP) field of the Identify Controller data structure is changed based on the requested capacity, the Capacity Adjustment Factor of the created Endurance Group, and the granularity at which the controller allocates NVM capacity; and 
b) the Endurance Group Identifier is added to the Endurance Group List.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 607, 815, 623]]<|/det|>
If an Endurance Group is deleted, then the controller performs the following actions in sequence:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 629, 883, 732]]<|/det|>
1) the Endurance Group Identifier is removed from the Endurance Group List; 
2) if the Media Unit Status log page is supported, then the Endurance Group Identifier field is cleared to Oh in all Media Unit Status Descriptors, if any, that indicate the deleted Endurance Group; 
3) every NVM Set in the Endurance Group is deleted; and 
4) the value indicated by the Unallocated NVM Capacity (UNVMCAP) field of the Identify Controller data structure is increased by the value that was indicated by the Total Endurance Group Capacity (TEGCAP) field of the Endurance Group Information log page of the deleted Endurance Group.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 738, 883, 768]]<|/det|>
If any of the entities modified by the above sequence are accessed after the sequence begins and before it completes, then the results are indeterminate.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 775, 830, 790]]<|/det|>
If an NVM Set is created, then the controller performs the following actions as an atomic operation:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 797, 883, 870]]<|/det|>
a) the NVM Set Identifier is added to the NVM Set List; and 
b) the Unallocated Endurance Group Capacity indicated by the Endurance Group Information log page (refer to Figure 222) is decreased by the amount of capacity allocated to the NVM Set; the controller may allocate NVM capacity in units such that the requested size for an NVM Set may be rounded up to the next unit boundary.
2025-12-21 11:23:02 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:175] - Processing image start: out/output_png/0556_page.png
2025-12-21 11:23:07 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:182] - Processing image end: <|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 89, 690, 105]]<|/det|>
If an NVM Set is deleted, then the following actions are performed in sequence:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[144, 111, 590, 126]]<|/det|>
1) the NVM Set Identifier is removed from the NVM Set List;  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[142, 126, 883, 156]]<|/det|>
2) if the Media Unit Status log page is supported, then the NVM Set Identifier field is cleared to Oh in all Media Unit Status Descriptors, if any, that indicated the deleted NVM Set;  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[144, 156, 502, 170]]<|/det|>
3) for each namespace in the deleted NVM Set:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[200, 177, 883, 257]]<|/det|>
a. all commands targeting the namespace are handled as described for namespace deletion in section 8.1.16; 
b. the namespace identifier is removed from the Allocated Namespace ID list; 
c. the namespace is deleted; 
d. for each controller to which the namespace was attached when that namespace was deleted:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[263, 272, 883, 331]]<|/det|>
i. the namespace identifier is added to the Changed Attached Namespace List log page for that controller; and 
ii. an Attached Namespace Attribute Changed asynchronous event is generated by that controller as described in section 8.1.16.2;  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[200, 336, 883, 366]]<|/det|>
e. the namespace identifier is added to the Changed Allocated Namespace List log page for each controller in the NVM subsystem; and  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[200, 366, 883, 410]]<|/det|>
f. an Allocated Namespace Attribute Changed asynchronous event is generated for each controller that has Allocated Namespace Attribute Notices enabled as described in section 8.1.16.2;  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[173, 410, 204, 423]]<|/det|>
and  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[142, 431, 883, 462]]<|/det|>
4) the Unallocated Endurance Group Capacity indicated by the Endurance Group Information log page is increased by the amount of capacity formerly allocated to the NVM Set.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 468, 883, 498]]<|/det|>
If any of the entities modified by the above sequence are accessed after the sequence begins and before it completes, then the results are indeterminate.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 504, 881, 535]]<|/det|>
If an NVM Set is created or deleted, the value indicated by the Unallocated NVM Capacity (UNVMCAP) field of the Identify Controller data structure is not changed.  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 543, 408, 559]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.4.2 Fixed Capacity Management  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 568, 883, 627]]<|/det|>
A Media Unit represents a component of the underlying media in a domain. An implementation may choose to represent each die as a separate Media Unit; however, this is not required. A Media Unit is the smallest media component for which the controller reports wear information (refer to the Available Spare field and the Percentage Used field in the Media Unit Status Descriptor, Figure 260).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 634, 881, 665]]<|/det|>
Two or more I/O operations to a Media Unit at the same time may interfere with each other as they contend for resources internal to or shared by that Media Unit.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 670, 498, 686]]<|/det|>
A controller supporting Fixed Capacity Management:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[142, 692, 883, 810]]<|/det|>
a) shall support the Media Unit Status log page (refer to section 5.2.12.1.16); 
b) shall support Endurance Groups (refer to section 3.2.3); 
c) may support NVM Sets (refer to section 3.2.2); 
d) shall set the Fixed Capacity Management bit to '1' in the CTRATT field of the Identify Controller data structure (refer to Figure 328); 
e) shall support the Supported Capacity Configuration List log page (refer to section 5.2.12.1.17); and 
f) shall support the Select Capacity Configuration operation of the Capacity Management command (refer to section 5.2.3).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 817, 883, 861]]<|/det|>
Media Units are accessed by way of Channels that represent communication pathways that may be a source of contention. This information is reported to facilitate the composition of NVM Sets that minimize interference between independent writers competing for this type of resource.
2025-12-21 11:23:09 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:175] - Processing image start: out/output_png/0557_page.png
2025-12-21 11:23:15 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:182] - Processing image end: <|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 88, 753, 106]]<|/det|>
The host allocates the Media Units in a domain to Endurance Groups and NVM Sets by:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 110, 883, 172]]<|/det|>
1) reading the Supported Capacity Configuration List log page (refer to Figure 261) and selecting the desired configuration; and2) issuing a Capacity Management command specifying the Select Capacity Configuration operation and the Capacity Configuration Identifier of the desired configuration.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 176, 883, 222]]<|/det|>
Following successful completion of the command, each Media Unit is allocated to one Endurance Group and to one NVM Set. The resulting configuration of Media Units is reported by the Media Unit Status log page (refer to section 5.2.12.1.16).  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 229, 429, 246]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.4.3 Variable Capacity Management  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 253, 883, 286]]<|/det|>
Variable Capacity Management allows the dynamic creation and deletion of Endurance Groups and NVM Sets.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 290, 518, 308]]<|/det|>
A controller supporting Variable Capacity Management:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 312, 883, 457]]<|/det|>
a) may support the Media Unit Status log page; 
b) shall support Endurance Groups; 
c) may support NVM Sets; 
d) shall set the Variable Capacity Management bit to '1' in the CTRATT field of the Identify Controller data structure (refer to Figure 328); 
e) shall support the Create Endurance Group operation of the Capacity Management command; 
f) may support the Delete Endurance Group operation of the Capacity Management command; and 
g) if NVM Sets are supported:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[200, 436, 883, 512]]<|/det|>
a. shall support the Create NVM Set operation of the Capacity Management command; 
b. shall report non-zero values in the Total Endurance Group Capacity field and the Unallocated Endurance Group Capacity field in the Endurance Group Information log page (refer to Figure 222); and 
c. may support the Delete NVM Set operation of the Capacity Management command.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 516, 883, 563]]<|/det|>
If a controller supports the Delete Endurance Group operation of the Capacity Management command, then it shall set the Delete Endurance Group bit to '1' in the CTRATT field of the Identify Controller data structure.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 567, 883, 600]]<|/det|>
If a controller supports the Delete NVM Set operation of the Capacity Management command, then it shall set the Delete NVM Set bit to '1' in the CTRATT field of the Identify Controller data structure.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 605, 536, 622]]<|/det|>
A typical sequence of operations for allocating capacity is:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 626, 829, 687]]<|/det|>
1) determine the available capacities in each domain (refer to section 3.2.5); 
2) create Endurance Group with desired capacity (refer to section 5.2.3); 
3) create NVM Set with desired capacity in the Endurance Group (refer to section 5.2.3); and 
4) create namespace with desired capacity in the NVM Set (refer to section 5.2.21).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 692, 555, 709]]<|/det|>
A typical sequence of operations for deallocating capacity is:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 713, 771, 760]]<|/det|>
1) delete namespace that contains formatted storage, if any, (refer to section 5.2.21); 
2) delete NVM Set, if any, (refer to section 5.2.30); and 
3) delete Endurance Group (refer to section 5.2.3).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 764, 883, 812]]<|/det|>
If there is insufficient unallocated capacity in an Endurance Group for the controller to create an NVM Set, then the host can delete one or more NVM Sets in that Endurance Group and create the new NVM Set using some or all of the available capacity.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 816, 883, 863]]<|/det|>
If there is insufficient unallocated capacity in a domain for the controller to create an Endurance Group, then the host can delete one or more Endurance Groups in that domain and create a new Endurance Group using some or all of the available capacity.
