#### 8.1.6.1.2 Configurable Device Personality Changes (Informative)  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 113, 855, 131]]<|/det|>
This section shows examples of the usage of the Configurable Device Personality capability by a host.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[117, 135, 750, 152]]<|/det|>
The host is able to change the NVM subsystem personality using the following process:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 157, 884, 413]]<|/det|>
1. Validate that the Configurable Device Personality capability is supported by issuing a Get Log Page command to the Feature Identifiers Supported and Effects log page and checking that the Configurable Device Personality Feature ID and is supported. 
2. Submit a Get Log Page command specifying the Device Personalities log page (refer to section 5.2.12.1.28) to determine which personalities are supported. 
3. The host determines the settings of the personalities that need to be changed and the required new settings values for each personality. 
4. Ensure that all requirements to change the settings of that personality are met (refer to section 5.2.26.1.24). 
5. Submit a Set Features command specifying the Configurable Device Personality feature, the Change Personality Settings (CHPS) bit set to '1', and that personality. If that Set Features command is processed successfully, then the change to the settings of the specified personality take effect in the NVM subsystem: a. before the successful completion of the Set Features command that requested that change if the personality does not require a reset; or b. prior to the successful completion of one of the resets required by that personality on the controller that processed the command, if the personality requires a reset.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 418, 883, 464]]<|/det|>
A host is able to unfreeze a personality if an authenticated unfreeze method is supported for that personality as described in section 8.1.6.2. A frozen personality that does not support an authentication method is not able to be unfrozen.  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 472, 448, 489]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.6.2 Authenticated Unfreeze Overview  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 497, 883, 528]]<|/det|>
Security Send and Security Receive commands, that specify CDP Authentication, are used to submit authenticated unfreeze operations to the controller and to retrieve the result of that request.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 533, 883, 564]]<|/det|>
Security Send and Security Receive commands populate the command data buffer with the data frames as defined in this section.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 570, 786, 587]]<|/det|>
A controller that supports version N of the data frames defined in Figure 630 and Figure 631:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 592, 884, 725]]<|/det|>
a) shall not generate an error for any value in the Request Data Frame Version field as a result of parsing a data frame with a different version than the version of the Request Data Frame data structure supported by the NVM subsystem (e.g., the controller shall not fail a command due to a version mismatch); 
b) shall process the data frames as defined by version N of these data frames regardless of the value in the Request Data Frame Version field; 
c) shall ignore non-zero values in fields that are reserved in version N of these data frames; and 
d) shall not perform any functionality related to these data frames that are not defined by version N of these data frames.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 730, 883, 805]]<|/det|>
For example, a controller, supporting version N of any request data frames that receives version N- 1 of these data frames from the host, processes the data frames provided for version N as described in this section. A controller, supporting version N of the CDP Request Data Frame and CDP Response Data Frame that receives version N+1 of these data frames from the host, processes the provided data frames for version N as described in this section.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 811, 883, 886]]<|/det|>
A host is able to unfreeze a single personality (refer to section 8.1.6.2.2) that supports an authenticated unfreeze method (refer to the Authenticated Unfreeze Support (AUS) field in Figure 285) by submitting a Security Send command requesting to unfreeze that personality (i.e., by setting the PERID field in the Request Data (REQD) field in the CDP Request Data Frame data structure (refer to Figure 630) to a supported PERID (refer to Figure 447)).
2025-12-21 11:23:54 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:175] - Processing image start: out/output_png/0563_page.png
2025-12-21 11:23:59 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:182] - Processing image end: <|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 90, 883, 163]]<|/det|>
A host is able to unfreeze all personalities that support a given authenticated unfreeze method by submitting a Security Send command requesting to unfreeze all personalities that support that authentication method by setting the PERID field in the REQD field in the CDP Request Data Frame data structure to FFh and setting the CDP Authentication Algorithm Method Identifier (CDPAMI) field in the CDP Unfreeze Request Data Frame to the value of that authentication method (refer to 8.1.6.2.1).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 169, 883, 243]]<|/det|>
An authentication command sequence consists of one or more Security Send and Security Receive commands specifying the NVMe Security Protocol and using CDP Authentication (refer to section 5.2.24.3). The concurrent processing of an independent authentication command sequence shall be supported for each Admin Submission Queue and Management Endpoint (refer to the NVM Express Management Interface Specification).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 249, 883, 323]]<|/det|>
If a Security Send command is processed requesting to unfreeze a personality that specifies an authentication method that is not supported by that personality (refer to the CDPALG field in Figure 328), then the controller returns a successful completion for that Security Send command, and the personality remains frozen. A controller processing a Security Receive command following that Security Send command in this sequence shall return a:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 330, 883, 376]]<|/det|>
- successful completion for the most recent successfully processed Security Receive command; and- CDP Response Data Frame, as defined in Figure 631, containing a Response Message Type with a value of 04h (i.e., Authentication Method Not Supported) and the corresponding Response Data.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 382, 495, 397]]<|/det|>
It is not an error to attempt to unfreeze a personality:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 406, 470, 436]]<|/det|>
- that is unfrozen; or- for an unsupported Personality Identifier.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 442, 883, 472]]<|/det|>
Security Send and Security Receive commands specifying CDP Authentication (i.e., a SPSP value of 0002h) do not use the NSID field and the NSID field shall be ignored by the controller.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 479, 883, 523]]<|/det|>
