The PSD data structure for a prohibited power state is removed from the Identify Controller data structure. If a prohibited power state becomes enabled (e.g., as a consequence of removing a power limit), then the PSD data structure for that power state is restored. In both cases, the power states that are enabled for use are renumbered so that the power states are contiguously numbered starting with zero with the maximum power of each subsequent power state being less than or equal to the maximum power of the previous power state, as described in section 8.1.18.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 183, 882, 243]]<|/det|>
Power state entries in the Autonomous Power State Transition table are renumbered as a result of the Power Limit feature changing the PSD table. The Autonomous Power State Transition table is modified to remove entries for prohibited power states and the Idle Transition Power State field in each remaining entry to indicate the new number of that power state that resulted from modification of the Power Limit feature.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[116, 249, 354, 264]]<|/det|>
This feature is used by a host to:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 272, 882, 362]]<|/det|>
- establish a power limit or reduce the value of a power limit, each of which may cause the controller to prohibit power states from use and remove PSD data structures (refer to section 8.1.18.6.1);- remove a power limit or increase the value of a power limit, each of which may cause the controller to restore PSD data structures and enable power states for use (refer to section 8.1.18.6.2); and- change the value of a power limit where that change has no effect on either the power states that are prohibited from use or the power states that are enabled for use (refer to section 8.1.18.6.3).  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[135, 370, 836, 387]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.18.6.1 Establishing or Reducing a Power Limit and Prohibiting Power States from Use  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 394, 882, 469]]<|/det|>
If controller processing of the Power Limit feature (e.g., processing of a Set Features command for this feature or processing of a Controller Level Reset) causes at least one enabled power state to become prohibited from use (i.e., results in a power limit that is less than the current value of the Maximum Power (MP) field in the Power State 0 Descriptor (PSD0) field in the Identify Controller data structure (refer to Figure 328)), then the controller shall perform the following steps in order:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[144, 475, 882, 534]]<|/det|>
1. if there is no current power limit in force (i.e., the current value of the PLV attribute of the Power Limit feature is cleared to Oh, refer to section 5.2.26.1.25) or the controller has removed a power limit as part of the power limit processing specified in section 8.1.18.6.2, then retain the following data for use as specified in section 8.1.18.6.2:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[201, 541, 882, 643]]<|/det|>
a. the entire set of PSD data structures; 
b. the entire set of entries in the Autonomous Power State Transition (APST) data structure (refer to Figure 411); 
c. the Number of Power States Support (NPSS) value in the Identify Controller data structure; and 
d. the two or three values (refer to section 4.4) of the power state attribute of the Power Management feature;  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[140, 650, 882, 680]]<|/det|>
2. prohibit the use of all power states whose maximum power (indicated by the MP field in the PSD for that power state) is greater than the specified power limit;  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 680, 535, 695]]<|/det|>
3. for each power state prohibited in step 2, remove:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[200, 701, 750, 731]]<|/det|>
a. the PSD data structure from the Identify Controller data structure; and 
b. the APST data structure entry (refer to Figure 412) for that power state;  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 738, 882, 810]]<|/det|>
4. shift the remaining set of PSD data structures (in step 3a) to lower byte offsets in the Identify Controller data structure so that the PSD0 field contains the PSD for the lowest numbered power state whose MP field value is less than or equal to that new power limit, and adjust the value of the Number of Power States Support (NPSS) field to match the number of remaining PSD data structures;  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 811, 882, 855]]<|/det|>
5. shift the remaining set of entries in the APST data structure (in step 3b) to match the shift of the PSD data structures in step 4, so that the first entry aligns with the new PSO after the power limit is applied;  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 855, 298, 869]]<|/det|>
6. clear to zero the:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[200, 869, 882, 899]]<|/det|>
a. PSD data structures in the Identify Controller data structure that no longer contain a usable PSD as a result of the changes made in step 3(a); and
