<|ref|>sub_title<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 521, 719, 539]]<|/det|>
### 8.1.30 Universally Unique Identifiers (UUIDs) for Vendor Specific Information  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 546, 586, 563]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.30.1 UUIDs for Vendor Specific Information Introduction  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 571, 882, 633]]<|/det|>
Several commands send or receive information that contains fields described as Vendor Specific or that is specified by a command field containing a value in a vendor specific range. Examples include the Set Features command, which may specify a vendor specific feature identifier, and the Identify command, which may retrieve a data structure having a vendor specific area.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 637, 882, 698]]<|/det|>
The vendor specific information may have different definitions (e.g., a vendor specific log page identifier with the contents of the page defined differently by different entities, such as an NVM subsystem vendor and an NVM subsystem customer). By associating each definition of the information with a UUID specified by the defining entity, a command is able to specify the particular definition of the information.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 702, 882, 763]]<|/det|>
A command specifies a particular definition of the information by specifying an index into a list of UUIDs supported by the controller (refer to section 5.2.13.2.14). The NVMe Invalid UUID (refer to section 8.1.30.2) is used to replace a previously valid UUID in the UUID List (refer to Figure 336). This is done to keep the values in the list at a static index, as that index is used by the Host to access the UUID List contents.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 768, 879, 799]]<|/det|>
NVM subsystem vendors and customers communicate (by means outside the scope of this specification) the UUID used for each definition of the information.  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 806, 600, 824]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.30.2 UUIDs for Vendor Specific Information Requirements  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 831, 882, 892]]<|/det|>
A UUID list is a list of non- zero UUID values, terminated by a 0h UUID value. Each non- zero UUID value may be either a valid UUID or the NVMe Invalid UUID. The NVMe Invalid UUID is the hexadecimal value FFFFFFFF_FFFFFFF_7FFFFFFF_FFFFFFF. A valid UUID is any non- zero value other than the NVMe Invalid UUID.
2025-12-21 11:41:03 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:175] - Processing image start: out/output_png/0680_page.png
2025-12-21 11:41:09 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:182] - Processing image end: <|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 89, 882, 134]]<|/det|>
If a command supports selection of a UUID, then the UUID Selection Supported bit in the Commands Supported and Effects data structure for that command (refer to Figure 214) shall be set to '1'. If a command does not support selection of a UUID, then the UUID Selection Supported bit shall be cleared to '0'.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 140, 882, 185]]<|/det|>
If the UUID Selection Supported bit is set to '1' for one or more commands, then the UUID List bit in the Controller Attributes field shall be set to '1' (refer to Figure 328), and the controller shall support reporting of a UUID List (refer to Figure 336).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 191, 882, 221]]<|/det|>
If a command supports selection of a UUID, then that command contains a UUID Index field (refer to Figure 716).  

<|ref|>table<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 259, 880, 316]]<|/det|>
<|ref|>table_caption<|/ref|><|det|>[[385, 235, 610, 251]]<|/det|>
Figure 716:UUID Index Field   

<table><tr><td>Bits</td><td>Description</td></tr><tr><td>6:0</td><td>UUID Index (UIDX): If this field is set to a non-zero value, then the value of this field is the index of a UUID in the UUID List (refer to Figure 336) that is used by the command. If this field is cleared to 0h, then no UUID index is specified.</td></tr></table>  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 329, 882, 388]]<|/det|>
If the UUID Index field specifies a valid UUID (i.e., the UUID Index field is set to a non- zero value and the UUID at that index indicates a valid UUID) (refer to section 5.2.13.2.14), then the controller shall process the command using the vendor specific information specified by that UUID. If the UUID Index field is cleared to 0h, then the command does not specify a UUID.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 394, 882, 424]]<|/det|>
If no UUID is specified by the command, then the controller shall process the command, returning vendor specific information.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 430, 761, 446]]<|/det|>
The controller shall abort the command with a status code of Invalid Field in Command if:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 452, 882, 497]]<|/det|>
a) The controller does not support the UUID specified by the UUID Index for the specified information; 
b) The UUID specified by the UUID Index is cleared to 0h; or 
c) The UUID specified by the UUID Index is the NVMe Invalid UUID.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 504, 882, 576]]<|/det|>
If a firmware image is activated that has a UUID List in which an entry is different from that of the previouslyactive firmware image, then a host that is unaware of the change may issue a command with the UUID index value for that entry. Such a command may produce unexpected results because the UUID specified by that UUID Index has changed. To avoid this, vendors should follow the following revision guidelines for UUID lists when constructing firmware images that support UUID selection:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 583, 882, 687]]<|/det|>
a) Add UUIDs that are not supported in prior firmware image revisions to the end of the UUID List in subsequent firmware image revisions; 
b) Remove UUIDs that are supported in prior firmware image revisions by replacing the UUID with the NVMe Invalid UUID in the same entry in the UUID list in subsequent firmware image revisions; 
c) Do not replace the NVMe Invalid UUID with a valid UUID in the same UUID list entry in subsequent firmware image revisions; and 
d) Do not shorten or remove the UUID list in subsequent firmware image revisions.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 693, 882, 723]]<|/det|>
In these guidelines, the terms "prior" and "subsequent" refer to a linear sequence of firmware versions (e.g., based on the date and time of the construction of the downloadable firmware image).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 730, 882, 788]]<|/det|>
Following these guidelines prevents the host from inadvertently specifying the wrong UUID because there is at most one valid UUID for each entry in the UUID list. Hence a command that specifies a UUID Index either specifies the intended UUID or is aborted because that entry in the UUID list is empty or contains the NVMe Invalid UUID.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 796, 882, 869]]<|/det|>
The controller shall require a reset to activate a downloaded firmware image (refer to section 5.2.8) if the downloaded image reports a UUID list with at least one slot in which a valid UUID replaces the NVMe Invalid UUID or a different valid UUID in the existing image. All controllers that are affected by the UUID list change caused by activation of a downloaded firmware image shall be reset as part of activating that downloaded firmware image.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 876, 882, 906]]<|/det|>
The above requirements for a reset to activate a downloaded firmware image do not require the controller to directly compare the UUID lists in the current and downloaded firmware images. For example, a vendor
2025-12-21 11:41:11 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:175] - Processing image start: out/output_png/0681_page.png
2025-12-21 11:41:17 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:182] - Processing image end: <|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 89, 883, 150]]<|/det|>
could use a vendor- specific major.minor firmware image revision numbering system (e.g., 3.5, 4.1) where all downloadable firmware images with the same major revision number follow the above guidelines. In that scenario, the controller is able to meet these reset requirements by requiring a reset if the downloaded firmware image and the currently executing firmware have different major revision numbers.  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 157, 567, 174]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.30.3 UUIDs for Vendor Specific Information Examples  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 182, 758, 198]]<|/det|>
This section includes examples of the use of UUIDs to select vendor specific information.  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[137, 206, 494, 223]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.30.3.1 Vendor Specific Log Page Example  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 231, 883, 276]]<|/det|>
If entity C and entity V create different definitions for a vendor specific log page having the same log page identifier (e.g., D0h), then each assigns a UUID to distinguish their definition (e.g., entity V assigns UUID V and entity C assigns UUID C).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 282, 513, 298]]<|/det|>
A controller supporting both definitions of the log page:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[142, 303, 883, 379]]<|/det|>
a) Sets the UUID List bit to '1' in the CTRATT field of the Identify Controller data structure (refer to Figure 328); 
b) Sets the UUID Selection Supported bit to '1' in the Commands Supported and Effects data structure (refer to Figure 214) corresponding to the Get Log Page command; and 
c) Reports both UUID V and UUID C in the UUID list (refer to Figure 336).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 385, 481, 401]]<|/det|>
A host requesting the log page defined by entity C:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 406, 759, 452]]<|/det|>
1) Determines the index of UUID C in the UUID list; 
2) Sets the Log Page Identifier field of the Get Log Page command to D0h; and 
3) Sets the UUID Index field of the Get Log Page command to the index of UUID C.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 458, 481, 474]]<|/det|>
A host requesting the log page defined by entity V:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 479, 759, 525]]<|/det|>
1) Determines the index of UUID V in the UUID list; 
2) Sets the Log Page Identifier field of the Get Log Page command to D0h; and 
3) Sets the UUID Index field of the Get Log Page command to the index of UUID V.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 530, 883, 576]]<|/det|>
A host not specifying the definition of the log page clears the UUID Index field to 0h. The selection of the log page definition returned by the controller is vendor specific (e.g., the controller may select any definition for the returned data).  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[137, 584, 481, 600]]<|/det|>
#### 8.1.30.3.2 Vendor Specific Feature Example  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 608, 883, 653]]<|/det|>
If entity C and entity V create different definitions for a vendor specific feature having the same Feature Identifier (e.g., F1h), then each assigns a UUID to distinguish their definitions (e.g., entity V assigns UUID V and entity C assigns UUID C).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 660, 728, 676]]<|/det|>
A controller supporting both definitions of the feature for the Get Features command:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[142, 681, 883, 787]]<|/det|>
a) Sets the UUID List bit to '1' in the CTRATT field of the Identify Controller data structure (refer to Figure 328); 
b) Sets the UUID Selection Supported bit to '1' in the Commands Supported and Effects data structure (refer to Figure 214) corresponding to the Get Features command; 
c) Sets the UUID Selection Supported bit to '1' in the FID Supported and Effects log page (refer to Figure 267); and 
d) Reports both UUID V and UUID C in the UUID list (refer to Figure 336).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 792, 580, 807]]<|/det|>
A host retrieving the attributes of the feature defined by entity C:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 814, 754, 859]]<|/det|>
1) Determines the index of UUID C in the UUID list; 
2) Sets the Feature Identifier field of the Get Features command to F1h; and 
3) Sets the UUID Index field of the Get Features command to the index of UUID C.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 865, 580, 881]]<|/det|>
A host retrieving the attributes of the feature defined by entity V:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 887, 528, 902]]<|/det|>
1) Determines the index of UUID V in the UUID list;
