<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[135, 181, 533, 198]]<|/det|>
#### 8.3.5.5.7 DH-HMAC-CHAP Security Requirements  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 206, 882, 310]]<|/det|>
In order to authenticate with the DH- HMAC- CHAP protocol, each host or controller uses a DH- HMACCHAP key that is associated with the entity's NQN. A DH- HMAC- CHAP key is unidirectional (i.e., used only for one direction of an authentication transaction). A DH- HMAC- CHAP key should not be associated with more than one NQN as this opens security vulnerabilities. All DH- HMAC- CHAP implementations should check for use of the same key with more than one NQN and should generate an administrative warning if this situation occurs (e.g., as a result of configuring a DH- HMAC- CHAP key to verify authentication of another entity).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[117, 315, 879, 347]]<|/det|>
The DH- HMAC- CHAP key is derived from an administratively configured secret (refer to section 8.3.5.5.8). Each host and NVM subsystem shall support:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 353, 882, 473]]<|/det|>
- transforming the provided secret into a key applying the HMAC function using the hash function specified in the secret representation (refer to section 8.3.5.5.8) with the secret as HMAC key to the concatenation of its own NQN not including the null terminator and the seventeen ASCII characters "NVMe-over-Fabrics" (i.e., key = HMAC(secret, NQN || "NVMe-over-Fabrics")). This transformation ensures the resulting key is uniquely associated with the entity identified by the NQN; and- using the provided secret as a key. This is intended for use with key management solutions able to ensure that key is uniquely associated with the entity identified by the NQN.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 478, 882, 538]]<|/det|>
NVM subsystems should support the ability to use a different NVM subsystem key with each host. Hosts should support the ability to use a different host key with each NVM subsystem. NVM subsystems should support the ability to use a different NVM subsystem secret with each host. Hosts should support the ability to use a different host secret with each NVM subsystem.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 543, 879, 575]]<|/det|>
If an implementation of NVMe over Fabrics is capable of functioning as both a host and an NVM subsystem, then that implementation shall use either:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 580, 861, 612]]<|/det|>
- one NQN for the host functionality and a different NQN for the NVM subsystem functionality; or- one NQN for both host functionality and NVM subsystem functionality.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 617, 882, 693]]<|/det|>
DH- HMAC- CHAP implementations may reuse a DH exponential (e.g., \(\mathfrak{g}^x\) mod p or \(\mathfrak{g}^y\) mod p). The primary risk in allowing reuse of a DH exponential is replay of a prior authentication sequence based on the attacker reusing the other exponential. For DH- HMAC- CHAP, replay is prevented with extremely high probability by the requirement that all challenges be randomly generated. Refer to section 2.12 of RFC 7296 for guidance on DH exponential reuse.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 698, 882, 744]]<|/det|>
The security of the DH- HMAC- CHAP protocol requires secrets, challenges, and DH exponents (i.e., x and y) to be generated from actual randomness. For a discussion of randomness and sources of randomness, refer to RFC 4086.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 750, 882, 810]]<|/det|>
Implementations shall use a cryptographic random number generator that should be seeded with at least 256 bits of entropy to generate random numbers for this protocol. The secret provisioning mechanism for each host and controller is outside of scope of this specification. For instance, secrets could be provisioned via an encrypted HTTPS- based connection.  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[137, 818, 395, 834]]<|/det|>
#### 8.3.5.5.8 Secret Representation  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 842, 882, 887]]<|/det|>
In order to facilitate provisioning, management, and interchange (e.g., copy & paste in an administrative configuration tool) of secrets, all NVMe over Fabrics entities shall support the following ASCII representation of secrets:
