DHHC- 1:xx:<Base64 encoded string>:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 112, 169, 126]]<|/det|>
Where:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 134, 883, 256]]<|/det|>
DHHC- 1:xx:<Base64 encoded string>:Where:- "DHHC- 1" indicates this is a version 1 representation of a secret for the DH-HMAC-CHAP protocol;- ': is used both as a separator and a terminator;- xx indicates the hash function to be used to transform the secret in key (refer to section 8.3.5.5.7), encoded as the ASCII representation of the hexadecimal value specified in Figure 798 (e.g., the two ASCII characters "01" indicate SHA- 256). The two ASCII characters "00" indicate no transform (i.e., use the secret as a key); and- The Base64 (refer to RFC 4648) string encodes the secret (32, 48, or 64 bytes binary) followed by the CRC- 32 (refer to RFC 1952) of the secret (4 bytes binary).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 261, 370, 276]]<|/det|>
As an example, the 32- byte secret:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[159, 281, 834, 298]]<|/det|>
89AEB31A 874EAF84 841B4673 6B0DFDF2 BA58D30A A2A545A3 E235A352 1E07594Ch  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 303, 883, 335]]<|/det|>
has the CRC- 32 A70D69FAh, that is represented in little endian format (i.e., FA690DA7h) for concatenation to the secret, resulting in the following:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[215, 340, 778, 357]]<|/det|>
"DHHC- 1:00:ia6zGodOr4SEG0Zzaw398rpY0wqipUWj4jWjUh4HWUz6aQ2n:"  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 363, 504, 379]]<|/det|>
when intended to be used as a key without transform.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 385, 883, 430]]<|/det|>
When provided with a secret in this format, NVMe over Fabrics entities shall verify the validity of the provided secret by computing the CRC- 32 value of the secret and checking the computed value with the provided value. If they do not match, then the secret shall not be used.  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[138, 438, 402, 454]]<|/det|>
#### 8.3.5.5.9 Generated PSK for TLS  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 462, 883, 566]]<|/det|>
When used with a non- NULL DH exchange, the DH- HMAC- CHAP protocol is able to generate a session key \(\mathsf{Ks}\) used to generate a pre- shared key (PSK) to establish a secure channel session with the TLS protocol between host and controller for NVMe/TCP. A TLS session is concatenated to an authentication transaction when the SC_C indication is set to NEWTLSPSK in the AUTH_Negotiate message (refer to section 8.3.5.1). A TLS session shall not be concatenated to an authentication transaction if the involved host and controller are administratively configured with a PSK for use with each other. In this case, host and controller shall only establish a TLS session based on the retained PSK derived from that configured PSK.  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 572, 883, 633]]<|/det|>
The session key \(\mathsf{Ks}\) shall be computed from the ephemeral DH key (i.e., \(\mathsf{g}^{\mathsf{xy}}\mathsf{mod}\mathsf{p}\) ) generated during the DH- HMAC- CHAP transaction by applying the hash function \(\mathsf{H}(\cdot)\) selected by the HashID parameter in the DH- HMAC- CHAP_Challenge message (i.e., \(\mathsf{Ks} = \mathsf{H}(\mathsf{g}^{\mathsf{xy}}\mathsf{mod}\mathsf{p})\) ). The size of the session key \(\mathsf{Ks}\) is determined by the selected hash function, as shown in Figure 798. Specifically:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 638, 883, 730]]<|/det|>
The host computes \(\mathsf{Ks}\) as the hash of the ephemeral DH key resulting from the combination of the random value y selected by the host with the DH exponential (i.e., \(\mathsf{g}^{\mathsf{xy}}\mathsf{mod}\mathsf{p}\) ) received from the controller (i.e., \(\mathsf{Ks} = \mathsf{H}((\mathsf{g}^{\mathsf{xy}}\mathsf{mod}\mathsf{p})^{\mathsf{y}}\mathsf{mod}\mathsf{p}) = \mathsf{H}(\mathsf{g}^{\mathsf{xy}}\mathsf{mod}\mathsf{p}))\) . The controller computes \(\mathsf{Ks}\) as the hash of the ephemeral DH key resulting from the combination of the random value \(\mathsf{x}\) selected by the controller with the DH exponential (i.e., \(\mathsf{g}^{\mathsf{xy}}\mathsf{mod}\mathsf{p}\) ) received from the host (i.e., \(\mathsf{Ks} = \mathsf{H}((\mathsf{g}^{\mathsf{xy}}\mathsf{mod}\mathsf{p})^{\mathsf{x}}\mathsf{mod}\mathsf{p}) = \mathsf{H}(\mathsf{g}^{\mathsf{xy}}\mathsf{mod}\mathsf{p}))\)  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 735, 883, 823]]<|/det|>
The generated PSK for TLS shall be computed applying the HMAC function using the hash function \(\mathsf{H}(\cdot)\) selected by the HashID parameter in the DH- HMAC- CHAP_Challenge message with the session key \(\mathsf{Ks}\) as key to the concatenation of the two challenges \(\mathsf{C}_1\) and \(\mathsf{C}_2\) (i.e., generated \(\mathsf{PSK} = \mathsf{HMAC}(\mathsf{Ks}, \mathsf{C}_1 \| \mathsf{C}_2))\) . This generated PSK should be replaced periodically (e.g., every hour) or on demand by performing a reauthentication on the Admin queue of the association with the appropriate SC_C value (refer to section 8.3.5.1).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 829, 883, 889]]<|/det|>
The host may request secure channel concatenation with the TLS protocol by setting the SC_C field in the AUTH_Negotiate message to NEWTLSPSK while performing only unidirectional authentication. In this case the host shall still send a challenge value \(\mathsf{C}_2\) to the controller and clear the sequence number \(\mathsf{S}_2\) to 0h to indicate that controller authentication is not requested.
