<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 459, 580, 477]]<|/det|>
#### 8.3.5.6 DH-HMAC-CHAP Authentication Verification Entity  

<|ref|>title<|/ref|><|det|>[[137, 485, 295, 501]]<|/det|>
#### 8.3.5.6.1 Overview  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 509, 883, 569]]<|/det|>
A DH- HMAC- CHAP Authentication Verification Entity (AVE) is a service that performs the DH- HMAC- CHAP authentication verification function on behalf of an NVMe entity (i.e., a host or a controller). An example of a DH- HMAC- CHAP authentication transaction with AVE is shown in Figure 812, using the notation shown in Figure 796.
2025-12-21 11:50:46 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:175] - Processing image start: out/output_png/0762_page.png
2025-12-21 11:50:50 - ocr_processor - [32mINFO[0m - [deepseek_ocr.py:182] - Processing image end: <|ref|>image<|/ref|><|det|>[[151, 117, 852, 496]]<|/det|>
<|ref|>image_caption<|/ref|><|det|>[[195, 89, 800, 107]]<|/det|>
<center>Figure 812: Example of DH-HMAC-CHAP authentication transaction with AVE </center>  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 508, 883, 613]]<|/det|>
As shown in Figure 812, a controller using the AVE service delegates to the AVE the verification of the response \(\mathsf{R}_1\) received from the host by passing the relevant DH- HMAC- CHAP authentication transaction parameters to the AVE through a DH- HMAC- CHAP_Access- Request message. A host using the AVE service delegates to the AVE the verification of the response \(\mathsf{R}_2\) received from the controller by passing the relevant DH- HMAC- CHAP authentication transaction parameters to the AVE through a DH- HMAC- CHAP_Access- Request message. In both cases, the AVE replies with a DH- HMAC- CHAP_Access- Result message containing the result of the authentication verification (refer to section 8.3.5.6.3).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 618, 883, 693]]<|/det|>
Use of the AVE service by an NVMe entity is optional and is determined by configuration of the NVMe entity. If an NVMe entity uses the AVE, then provisioning of DH- HMAC- CHAP information on that entity is reduced to only that entity's DH- HMAC- CHAP secret (refer to section 8.3.5.5.8) and the parameters (refer to section 8.3.5.6.2) for accessing the AVE (i.e., no DH- HMAC- CHAP keys are required to be provisioned for verification of responses received from other NVMe entities).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 698, 682, 715]]<|/det|>
An AVE is required to maintain the following information for each NVMe entity:  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[143, 721, 654, 768]]<|/det|>
The NQN of that entity (i.e., \(\mathsf{NQN}_{\mathsf{e}}\) - the DH- HMAC- CHAP key associated with that entity (i.e., \(\mathsf{K}_{\mathsf{e}}\) ); and the PSK shared between that entity and the AVE (i.e., \(\mathsf{PSK}_{\mathsf{ea}}\) ).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 774, 883, 820]]<|/det|>
\(\mathsf{K}_{\mathsf{e}}\) is used to perform the authentication verification function (refer to section 8.3.5.6.3) and \(\mathsf{PSK}_{\mathsf{ea}}\) is used to establish a secure connection with the AVE (refer to section 8.3.5.6.2). An AVE shall support all hash functions defined for DH- HMAC- CHAP (refer to section 8.3.5.5.2).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[114, 825, 883, 871]]<|/det|>
To facilitate dynamic discovery of the transport addresses of an AVE through a Discovery Controller (refer to section 8.3.5.6.4) and to simplify establishing a secure connection to an AVE (refer to section 8.3.5.6.2), an AVE is identified at by an NQN (NQNAVE).
